# No unsolicited legacy support

Never add legacy, backwards-compatibility, migration, fallback, or transitional support unless I explicitly ask for it.

When changing, refactoring, or redesigning code, assume that only the current code, data model, API shape, and contracts matter.

Do not preserve old behavior, old field names, old data formats, old APIs, old cache shapes, or deprecated code paths merely because they might still exist somewhere.

Do not add compatibility layers, fallback paths, adapters, dual-read/write logic, migrations, legacy detection, or transitional abstractions unless I explicitly request them.

If something is incompatible with the new design, change it to the new design. Do not make the new design accommodate the old one.

Prefer deleting obsolete code and simplifying the codebase over preserving it "just in case."

Only introduce backwards compatibility when I explicitly say that legacy support, backwards compatibility, migration, or support for an older version is required.

**Do not infer a need for legacy support.**
